WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.419
Teacher midterm practice for C plus plus, question 11.

2
00:00:05.039 --> 00:00:08.223
A student checks whether two five element arrays are equal.

3
00:00:08.223 --> 00:00:11.842
For A one equals one, two, three, four, five, and A two

4
00:00:11.842 --> 00:00:15.702
equals one, nine, three, four, five, what is printed?

5
00:00:17.402 --> 00:00:22.457
eq starts true. i equals zero: one equals one, so the else sets eq to true.

6
00:00:23.078 --> 00:00:27.539
i equals one: two is not nine, so eq becomes false. Good so far.

7
00:00:28.158 --> 00:00:33.024
But at i equals two, three equals three, and the else sets eq back to true!

8
00:00:33.024 --> 00:00:35.836
The same happens for i equals three and four.

9
00:00:36.457 --> 00:00:41.184
After the loop eq is true, so it prints A one and A two are equal. That is wrong.

10
00:00:42.784 --> 00:00:45.358
A flag search must only move one way.

11
00:00:45.358 --> 00:00:48.342
Start by assuming equal, and the only thing the

12
00:00:48.342 --> 00:00:51.092
loop may do is switch the flag to false.

13
00:00:51.092 --> 00:00:55.480
The else that resets it means only the last pair decides the result.

14
00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:01.542
So the program prints A one and A two are equal, because the flag gets reset.

15
00:01:02.362 --> 00:01:04.570
Adding an else that resets the flag.

16
00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:07.599
That is exactly the bug the teacher's hint is about.

17
00:01:08.318 --> 00:01:13.495
Exam tip: in flag loops, ask whether a later pass can undo an earlier result.

18
00:01:13.495 --> 00:01:16.083
If it can, the flag logic is wrong.
